%% Fact Book section for Topic 6 — included by content/FB/main.gsm under a "Topic 6" H1.

## T6 · Dates and milestones

::: table | Topic 6 timeline — suffrage, quotas and firsts.
@widths 1.8,12.2
@font 9.5
Year | Event
1792 | Wollstonecraft, *A Vindication of the Rights of Woman*
1848 | Seneca Falls Convention; Declaration of Sentiments
1866 | J. S. Mill's women's suffrage petition to Parliament
1869 | NWSA and AWSA founded; Wyoming gives women the vote; Mill, *The Subjection of Women*
1890 | NAWSA formed
1893 | New Zealand — first country to give women the vote (Kate Sheppard)
1897 | NUWSS (Millicent Fawcett)
1903 | WSPU (Emmeline Pankhurst) — "Deeds, not words"
1906 | Finland — women vote and stand; "suffragette" coined by the *Daily Mail*
1913 | Cat and Mouse Act; Emily Wilding Davison dies
1917 | Women's Indian Association deputation to Montagu
1918 | Britain — women over 30 with property
1920 | USA — 19th Amendment
1921 | Madras — first Indian province to enfranchise women
1928 | Britain — Equal Franchise Act (21)
1931 | Ceylon — universal adult franchise
1935 | Government of India Act — wider franchise and reserved seats for women
1947 | Pakistan — adult franchise; first Constituent Assembly with two women
1948 | UDHR Article 21
1956 | Constitution — 10 reserved seats (5 East, 5 West)
1960 | Sirimavo Bandaranaike — world's first woman prime minister
1962 | Constitution — 6 reserved seats
1965 | Fatima Jinnah contests the presidential election
1966 | ICCPR Article 25
1967 | Pitkin, *The Concept of Representation*
1970 | First direct nationwide general election in Pakistan
1973 | Constitution — 10 reserved seats for 10 years or three elections; Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan governor of Sindh
1977 | Kanter, *Men and Women of the Corporation* — tokens
1979 | CEDAW (Articles 4 and 7)
1985 | Reserved seats raised to 20
1988 | Benazir Bhutto first woman PM; last election with reserved seats before the lapse; Dahlerup on critical mass
1990 | ECOSOC 30% target
1990–97 | Three elections with no reserved seats
1992 | India's 73rd/74th Amendments — one-third local seats; World Bank, *Governance and Development*
1995 | Beijing Platform — Critical Area G; Phillips, *The Politics of Presence*
1996 | Pakistan accedes to CEDAW
2000 | Devolution of Power Plan (33%); NCSW; UNSCR 1325; France's parity law
2001 | Local Government Ordinance — about 36,000 women councillors
2002 | LFO — 60 NA, 17 Senate, 128 provincial seats; NA of 342; voting age 18; National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women
2003 | 17th Amendment validates the LFO
2004 | Chattopadhyay and Duflo on women panchayat leaders
2005 | Ryan and Haslam — the "glass cliff"
2007 | Benazir Bhutto assassinated (27 December); Eagly and Carli — the labyrinth
2008 | Fahmida Mirza first woman Speaker; Women's Parliamentary Caucus; BISP
2010 | 18th Amendment; ICCPR ratified; workplace harassment law
2011 | Hina Rabbani Khar first woman foreign minister; acid and anti-women practices laws; Bangladesh 50 reserved seats
2015 | Nepal's constitution (one-third); Lower Dir by-election annulled
2016 | Anti-honour-killing and anti-rape laws
2017 | Elections Act — 5% tickets (s. 206), 10% turnout rule (s. 9); Tehmina Janjua first woman foreign secretary
2018 | 25th Amendment — NA 336, 60 women's seats kept; 8 women win general NA seats
2022 | Justice Ayesha Malik first woman Supreme Court judge
2023 | India's Constitution (106th Amendment) Act — one-third after delimitation
2024 | Maryam Nawaz first woman chief minister; 306 women contest, 11 win general NA seats; Harini Amarasuriya Sri Lanka's PM; Hasina ousted in Bangladesh
2025 | No women federal ministers (WEF)
2026 | Bangladesh — 7 women directly elected, 57 of 350; Nepal — 96 of 275 women
:::

